<Base
  title="story | Tom Frodyma"
  description="How a kid on Skype, TeamSpeak, and Discord ended up building AI products at Change."
>
  <p><span class="nick">&lt;tom&gt;</span> lore drop incoming.</p>

  <h1>origin story</h1>

  <p>
    My dad had a plant nursery. &ldquo;Kwekerij&rdquo;, it's a Dutch word worth
    learning. His world was one you could touch. I grew up in a digital world,
    and never really left it. I like to think we did the same work in different
    soil: he raised seedlings, I raise products.
  </p>
  <p>
    From a young age there was a headset on my ears and a mic in front of my
    mouth. I was talking with people from all over the world before I had ever
    left the country.
  </p>
  <p>Skype first, then TeamSpeak, then Discord, where I still am today.</p>
  <p>
    Somewhere between those calls I started building websites. Small ones.
    Rough ones. Mine. What stuck was the feeling: you type something, you hit
    refresh, and there it is, real, in the browser. I have been chasing that
    feeling ever since. These days it has AI in the loop, but it's the same
    rush.
  </p>
  <p>
    I tried a graphic design degree. It made sense on paper: I liked making
    things, and design is making things. But something kept itching. I did not
    only want to make things look good. I wanted to build the things
    themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>the floor</h2>
  <p>
    After moving to the Czech Republic I found a remote job at Change, selling
    for the Marktplaats team. Unglamorous, and honestly a great product
    education: all day inside other people's problems, told in their own
    words.
  </p>
  <p>
    I kept building what my team needed. First a tracker so everyone logged
    their results in one place, then dashboards, then full applications. Each
    one pulled me a step closer to product.
  </p>
  <p>
    That led to product manager at Change AI, where I now build tools for the
    person I used to be. The full quest log is on the
    <a href="/work/">work page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>the gaming thing</h2>
  <p>
    I'm a gamer at heart, and I mean that as a work skill, not a fun fact on a
    slide.
  </p>
  <p>
    I have always played competitive games: Counter-Strike, Valorant, and
    plenty more. Climbing the ranks, playing as a team, and entering
    tournaments. A few things from that transfer directly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <span class="ok">learning loops:</span> games teach you to try, fail,
      see what went wrong, and go again. Product work is the same loop, just
      slower.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="ok">reading systems:</span> getting good at a game means
      figuring out how its rules work together. I do the same with a workflow:
      find out what really drives it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not every hour of gaming was useful. A lot of it was just fun. But the
    overlap is real, and I use it every week.
  </p>

  <p><span class="nick">&lt;tom&gt;</span> that's the lore. where next?</p>
